            <ArticleTitle>Bardet-Biedl syndrome and a large concha bullosa pyocele.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2316-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijporl.2014.10.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0165-5876(14)00567-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bardet-Biedl syndrome is a rare disease involving rode cone dystrophy, polydactyly, obesity, learning disabilities, hypogonadism and renal anomalies, symptoms caused by immotile cilia dysfunction. We report the first case of this syndrome in a teenager with an endonasal mass secondary to pyocele of a concha bullosa. The patient was treated successfully with endoscopic sinus surgery. Nasal obstruction secondary to a middle turbinate pyocele in Bardet-Biedl syndrome has not been described before. The pediatrician and the rhinologist should take this entity into consideration when investigating patients with Bardet-Biedl syndrome and sinonasal symptoms.</AbstractText>
